  m_Name: SurfaceCondenserData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Surface Condenser
  engineDescription: A Surface Condenser is a heat-transfer device used to condense
    steam or vapor into liquid by transferring its heat to a separate cooling fluid
    without allowing the two fluids to mix. Its main components include the condenser
    shell, tubes, tube sheets, cooling-water inlet and outlet connections, steam
    inlet, condensate outlet, air removal system, and supporting structure. During
    operation, exhaust steam enters the condenser and comes into contact with the
    outer surfaces of cooling tubes. Cooling water flows through the tubes and absorbs
    heat from the steam, causing the steam to condense into water. The resulting
    condensate collects at the bottom of the condenser and can be returned to the
    feedwater system. Surface condensers are widely used in steam power plants, marine
    propulsion systems, industrial process plants, and large refrigeration and thermal
    systems. From an engineering perspective, understanding condensation heat transfer,
    vacuum maintenance, cooling-water flow, pressure drop, air leakage, fouling,
    corrosion, tube integrity, and condensate recovery is essential for maintaining
    high thermal efficiency and reliable operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
